[s0l0!-header][h1]Loops in ECMAScript 6[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

In JavaScript we commonly use the [b]for [/b]loop to iterate over values in a list:[code format="js" codeId="1429"]let arr = [1, 2, 3];
[b]for[/b] (let k = 0; k < arr.length; k++) {
  console.log(arr[k]);
}[/code]
The [b]for...in[/b] loop is intended for iterating over the enumerable keys of an object.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1430"]let obj = {a: 1, b: 2, c: 3};
[b]for [/b](let v [b]in [/b]obj) {
  console.log(v);
}[/code]
[note]The [b]for...in[/b] loop should [b]NOT [/b]be used to iterate over arrays because, depending on the JavaScript engine, it could iterate in an arbitrary order. Also, the iterating variable is a [b]string[/b], not a number, so if you try to do any math with the variable, you'll be performing string concatenation instead of addition.[/note]
ES6 introduces the new [b]for...of[/b] loop, which creates a loop iterating over iterable objects.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="1177"]let list = ["x", "y", "z"];
[b]for[/b] (let val [b]of [/b]list) {
  console.log(val);
}[/code]
During each iteration the [b]val [/b]variable is assigned the corresponding element in the list.

The [b]for...of[/b] loop works for other iterable objects as well, including [b]strings[/b]:[code format="js" codeId="1166"][b]for[/b] (let ch [b]of [/b]"Hello") {
  console.log(ch);
}[/code]
[note]The [b]for...of[/b] loop also works on the newly introduced collections ([b]Map[/b], [b]Set[/b], [b]WeakMap[/b], and [b]WeakSet[/b]). We will learn about them in the upcoming lessons.
Note that ES6 code will run only in browsers that support it. Older devices and browsers that do not support ES6 will return a syntax error. [/note]
