[s0l0!-header][h1]Functions in ECMAScript 6[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Prior to ES6, a JavaScript function was defined like this: [code format="js" codeId="4884"][b]function [/b]add(x, y) {
  var sum = x+y;  
  console.log(sum);
}[/code]
ES6 introduces a new syntax for writing functions. The same function from above can be written as: [code format="js" codeId="4885"]const add = (x, y) => {
  let sum = x + y;  
  console.log(sum);
}[/code]
This new syntax is quite handy when you just need a simple function with one argument.
You can skip typing [b]function [/b]and [b]return[/b], as well as some parentheses and braces.
[s0l0!-header][b]For example:[/b][/s0l0!-header] [code format="js" codeId="4886"]const greet = x [b]=>[/b] "Welcome " + x;[/code]
The code above defines a function named [b]greet [/b]that has one argument and returns a message.

If there are no parameters, an empty pair of parentheses should be used, as in[code format="js" codeId="4887"]const x = [b]()[/b] => alert("Hi");[/code]
The syntax is very useful for inline functions. For example, let's say we have an array, and for each element of the array we need to execute a function. We use the [b]forEach [/b]method of the array to call a function for each element: [code format="js" codeId="4888"]var arr = [2, 3, 7, 8];

arr.forEach(function(el) {
  console.log(el * 2);
});[/code]
However, in ES6, the code above can be rewritten as following: [code format="js" codeId="4889"]const arr = [2, 3, 7, 8];

arr.forEach(v => {
  console.log(v * 2);
});[/code]
[note]The code is shorter and looks pretty nice, doesn't it? :)[/note]

